WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.812
Third Normal Form removes transitive dependencies.

00:00:02.812 --> 00:00:07.702
Even after separating appointment and service facts, we should not keep patient name and provider specialty

00:00:07.702 --> 00:00:13.482
inside every appointment row if those values are determined by patient or provider identifiers.

00:00:13.482 --> 00:00:17.507
The appointment should store the foreign keys that identify the patient and provider.

00:00:17.507 --> 00:00:19.622
Patient details belong in patient.

00:00:19.622 --> 00:00:21.897
Provider details belong in provider.

00:00:21.897 --> 00:00:26.027
A common memory aid for 3NF is that non-key facts should describe the key and should not really depend on

00:00:26.027 --> 00:00:28.897
another non-key attribute. The practical benefit is easier maintenance.

00:00:28.897 --> 00:00:35.217
If a provider's specialty changes, we update the provider record rather than many appointment rows.

00:00:35.217 --> 00:00:39.362
Connect this idea to a university system separating students, courses, sections, and enrollments.

00:00:39.362 --> 00:00:43.187
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:43.187 --> 00:00:47.767
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:47.767 --> 00:00:51.712
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:51.712 --> 00:00:57.120
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
